(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["pulmonary"]="<h1>Pulmonary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-two-circulations-in-one-lung\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Two circulations in one lung</h2>\n<p>The lung receives blood from <strong>two separate systems</strong>, and they do different jobs:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Pulmonary circulation</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Bronchial circulation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Job</td>\n  <td><strong>Gas exchange</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Nourishes</strong> the airways and supporting tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Comes from</td>\n  <td>Right ventricle → pulmonary arteries</td>\n  <td><strong>Aorta</strong> → bronchial arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure</td>\n  <td>Low</td>\n  <td>Systemic (high)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Drains to</td>\n  <td>Pulmonary veins → left atrium</td>\n  <td>Partly into the <strong>pulmonary veins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Why does that last row matter?</strong> Bronchial blood has already given its oxygen to the lung tissue. When some of it drains into the pulmonary veins, it mixes a little oxygen-poor blood into the freshly oxygenated blood heading for the left atrium. This is one reason arterial blood carries slightly less oxygen than the air in the alveoli.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Anatomical illustration of the pulmonary circuit showing right ventricular outflow to pulmonary arteries, gas exchange in lungs and pulmonary venous return to the left atrium\"><figcaption><strong>Pulmonary circuit anatomy.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2119_Pulmonary_Circuit.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-same-flow-much-lower-pressure\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Same flow, much lower pressure</h2>\n<p>The two sides of the heart are <strong>in series</strong>, so the right ventricle must pump the <strong>same output</strong> as the left: about 5 L/min at rest. Yet the pressures are very different:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Pulmonary</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Systemic</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Arterial pressure</td>\n  <td>About <strong>25/8</strong> mmHg (mean about 15)</td>\n  <td>About <strong>120/80</strong> mmHg (mean about 93)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure at the end of the circuit</td>\n  <td>Left atrium, about 5</td>\n  <td>Right atrium, about 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pressure drop across the circuit</td>\n  <td>About <strong>10</strong> mmHg</td>\n  <td>About <strong>90</strong> mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The same flow is pushed through by a pressure drop about <strong>one-ninth</strong> as large, so <strong>pulmonary resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic resistance</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/hemodynamics\">→ Haemodynamics</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why so low?</strong> The pulmonary vessels are <strong>short, wide, thin-walled and stretchy</strong>, and the capillary network is huge, with countless paths in parallel.</p>\n<p><strong>Exercise: more flow, little extra pressure.</strong> When cardiac output rises, two things happen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Recruitment:</strong> capillaries that were closed or barely perfused <strong>open up</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Distension:</strong> capillaries that were already open <strong>widen</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both lower the resistance, so pulmonary flow can rise several-fold while pulmonary arterial pressure rises only a little. That protects the thin capillaries, and the right ventricle, from high pressure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-the-alveoli-stay-dry\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Why the alveoli stay dry</h2>\n<p>The alveolar wall must be thin for gas exchange, so the lung can't afford fluid leaking into it. Compare the two main forces across a pulmonary capillary <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation\">→ Microcirculation</a>)</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Capillary <span data-term=\"hydrostatic-pressure\">hydrostatic pressure</span></strong>, pushing fluid out: only about <strong>10 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Plasma <span data-term=\"oncotic-pressure\">oncotic pressure</span></strong>, pulling fluid in: about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The outward push is unusually weak. So only a small amount of fluid filters into the lung's interstitium, and the <strong>lymphatics</strong> carry it away easily, long before it can reach the alveoli.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The full balance also includes the lung's interstitial forces (a slightly negative interstitial pressure and a fairly high interstitial protein level), which leave a small net outward filtration that the lymphatics clear. The lecture gives the two main values, 10 and 25 mmHg; Guyton and Hall give the full balance.</aside>\n\n<p><strong>When does this safety margin fail?</strong> In <strong>left heart failure</strong>, left atrial pressure rises and backs up into the pulmonary veins and capillaries. Once capillary hydrostatic pressure climbs past about <strong>25 mmHg</strong>, roughly the plasma oncotic pressure, filtration outruns lymph drainage. Fluid collects first in the interstitium, then floods the alveoli: <strong>pulmonary oedema</strong>, which thickens the barrier and impairs gas exchange. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure?section=3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up\">→ Heart Failure, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What is the main job of the bronchial circulation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. To carry blood from the right ventricle for gas exchange</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. To nourish the airways and supporting lung tissue</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. To return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. To drain lymph from the alveoli into the bloodstream</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"pulmonary:e4f9aecb\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The bronchial arteries come from the aorta and supply the airways. Gas exchange is the job of the pulmonary circulation.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> The right and left ventricles pump the same output, but pulmonary arterial pressure is far lower. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right ventricle pumps a smaller volume with each beat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Blood flows through the lungs only during diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pulmonary arteries contain valves that lower the pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary vascular resistance is only about one-tenth of systemic</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"pulmonary:e5aefbdd\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Same flow with a much smaller pressure drop means a much lower resistance: short, wide, stretchy vessels arranged in parallel.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> In left heart failure, what makes fluid flood the alveoli?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Plasma oncotic pressure rises and pulls fluid into the alveoli</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Bronchial arteries leak fluid directly into the alveolar spaces</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Pulmonary capillary pressure rises until filtration outruns lymph drainage</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls, so capillaries collapse</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"pulmonary:66e93f2e\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> High left atrial pressure backs up into the capillaries. Once the outward push beats the oncotic pull and the lymphatics, fluid accumulates.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-gravity-posture-and-the-lung-s-blood-reservoir\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Gravity, posture and the lung's blood reservoir</h2>\n<p><strong>Standing up:</strong> the lung apex sits above the heart and the base below it. Gravity adds pressure to the blood in the lower vessels and takes it away from the upper ones. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/gravity\">→ Gravity</a>)</em> So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At the <strong>base</strong>, vascular pressure is higher, more vessels are open and wider, and <strong>blood flow is greatest</strong>.</li>\n<li>At the <strong>apex</strong>, vascular pressure is lowest and <strong>blood flow is least</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>West zones: compare three pressures.</strong> Whether a capillary is open depends on the pressure inside it versus the <strong>alveolar air pressure</strong> squeezing it from outside:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Zone</th>\n  <th>Pressures</th>\n  <th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Zone 1</strong></td>\n  <td>Alveolar &gt; arterial &gt; venous</td>\n  <td>Capillaries squeezed shut: <strong>no flow</strong>. Not present in healthy lungs; can appear after haemorrhage or with positive-pressure ventilation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Zone 2</strong> (upper lung)</td>\n  <td>Arterial &gt; alveolar &gt; venous</td>\n  <td>Flow depends on <strong>arterial − alveolar</strong> pressure, so it is intermittent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Zone 3</strong> (lower lung)</td>\n  <td>Arterial &gt; venous &gt; alveolar</td>\n  <td>Capillaries always open: <strong>continuous flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>West zones are <strong>pressure relationships</strong>, not fixed stripes of lung. They shift with posture, blood pressure and breathing.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure dg-figure\"><figcaption>West zones in an upright lung</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An upright lung shaded from pale at the apex to dark at the base, showing blood flow increasing downwards. Zone 1 at the apex: alveolar pressure above arterial and venous, no flow; not present in healthy lungs. Zone 2: arterial above alveolar above venous, flow on and off. Zone 3 at the base: arterial above venous above alveolar, continuous and greatest flow.\"><defs><clipPath id=\"wz-lung\"><path d=\"M100 24C52 34 22 110 22 190C22 236 50 266 104 266C140 266 168 254 172 230V40C160 30 128 22 100 24Z\"/></clipPath></defs><g clip-path=\"url(#wz-lung)\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"200\" height=\"92\" class=\"dg-zone1\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"92\" width=\"200\" height=\"78\" class=\"dg-zone2\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"170\" width=\"200\" height=\"110\" class=\"dg-zone3\"/></g><path d=\"M100 24C52 34 22 110 22 190C22 236 50 266 104 266C140 266 168 254 172 230V40C160 30 128 22 100 24Z\" class=\"dg-outline\"/><path d=\"M24 92H350M22 170H350\" class=\"dg-guide\"/><text x=\"184\" y=\"44\" class=\"dg-b\">Zone 1 (apex)</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"62\" class=\"dg-s\">PA > Pa > Pv: no flow</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"80\" class=\"dg-s\">Not in healthy lungs</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"116\" class=\"dg-b\">Zone 2</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"134\" class=\"dg-s\">Pa > PA > Pv</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"152\" class=\"dg-s\">Flow on and off</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"200\" class=\"dg-b\">Zone 3 (base)</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"218\" class=\"dg-s\">Pa > Pv > PA</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"236\" class=\"dg-s\">Continuous, greatest</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"254\" class=\"dg-s\">flow</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"292\" class=\"dg-s\">PA alveolar · Pa arterial · Pv venous pressure</text></svg><p>Blood flow increases from apex to base because gravity raises vascular pressure lower in the lung. Zones are pressure relationships, not fixed stripes: they shift with posture, blood pressure and breathing.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Lying down:</strong> the whole lung is at about the level of the heart, so flow is spread more evenly. And about <strong>400 mL</strong> of blood moves from the body into the lungs. Standing up returns it to the circulation. So the pulmonary vessels act as a <strong>blood reservoir</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this matters: <span data-term=\"orthopnoea\">orthopnoea</span>.</strong> In left heart failure, the pulmonary capillaries are already near their limit. Lying flat adds that extra blood to the lungs, capillary pressure rises further, and the patient becomes <strong>breathless lying down</strong>. Sitting up moves blood back to the lower body and eases it. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/heart-failure?section=3-left-versus-right-failure-where-does-the-blood-back-up\">→ Heart Failure, §3</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-low-oxygen-constricts-lung-vessels\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Low oxygen constricts lung vessels</h2>\n<p>In most tissues, low oxygen <strong>dilates</strong> the arterioles, bringing in more blood. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Control</a>)</em> The lung does the <strong>opposite</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction:</strong> when an alveolus has low oxygen, the small pulmonary arteries supplying it <strong>constrict</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the opposite?</strong> In the lung, low oxygen in an alveolus means that alveolus is <strong>poorly ventilated</strong>. Sending blood there would waste it, because the blood can't pick up oxygen. Constricting those vessels <strong>diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli</strong>, matching blood flow to air flow (the <strong>ventilation–perfusion ratio</strong>). A rise in CO₂ (and H⁺) also constricts pulmonary vessels, which helps in the same way.</p>\n<p><strong>Local versus everywhere:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One poorly ventilated region</strong> (for example, a blocked airway): blood is redirected, and oxygenation improves.</li>\n<li><strong>Every alveolus hypoxic</strong> (high altitude, chronic lung disease): there is nowhere better to send the blood. Constriction everywhere <strong>raises total pulmonary resistance</strong>. If it persists, the result is <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>, and the right ventricle strains against it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Before birth</strong>, the same response keeps fetal pulmonary resistance high, so blood bypasses the fluid-filled lungs. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/fetal?section=1-why-does-the-fetus-need-shortcuts\">→ Fetal Circulation, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Nerves and <span data-term=\"nitric-oxide\">nitric oxide</span>:</strong> the pulmonary vessels have an extensive autonomic supply; sympathetic stimulation <strong>constricts</strong> them. <strong>Nitric oxide</strong> from the endothelium <strong>dilates</strong> them.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–5\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–5 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> In an upright person, why does the lung base receive more blood than the apex?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Gravity raises vascular pressure at the base, opening and widening vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Alveolar pressure is much higher at the base than at the apex</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The bronchial arteries supply the base but not the apex</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Hypoxic vasoconstriction opens the vessels at the base</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"pulmonary:65f282cd\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Below the heart, gravity adds pressure to the blood, so more capillaries are open and distended.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does a patient with left heart failure become breathless when lying flat?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Lying flat lowers the pressure in the pulmonary capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Lying flat raises the plasma oncotic pressure in the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The heart pumps less blood into the lungs when lying flat</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. About 400 mL of extra blood moves into the already congested lungs</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"pulmonary:a4c10978\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> The extra blood raises pulmonary capillary pressure further, pushing more fluid into the lung tissue. This is orthopnoea.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> At high altitude, why does pulmonary arterial pressure rise?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Low oxygen dilates pulmonary vessels, so more blood enters the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The bronchial arteries constrict and divert blood into the lungs</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Every alveolus is hypoxic, so vessels constrict throughout the lung</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Gravity has a stronger effect on the lungs at high altitude</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"pulmonary:15f43ecb\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> With hypoxia everywhere, there is no better-ventilated region to send blood to. Widespread constriction raises total resistance.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"6-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Two circulations:</strong> pulmonary (gas exchange, low pressure) and bronchial (from the aorta, nourishes the airways, partly drains into pulmonary veins).</li>\n<li><strong>Same output as the left side</strong> at about one-tenth of the resistance: mean pulmonary arterial pressure about 15 mmHg.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> recruitment and distension let flow rise with little rise in pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Alveoli stay dry:</strong> capillary hydrostatic pressure is about 10 mmHg against an oncotic pressure of about 25; lymphatics clear the small leak. Above about 25 mmHg capillary pressure (left heart failure), pulmonary oedema develops.</li>\n<li><strong>Upright:</strong> gravity raises vascular pressure at the base, so most flow goes there. <strong>West zones:</strong> zone 1 (alveolar &gt; arterial) no flow, not in healthy lungs; zone 2 (arterial &gt; alveolar &gt; venous) on-off flow; zone 3 (both vascular pressures &gt; alveolar) continuous flow.</li>\n<li><strong>Lying down</strong> moves about 400 mL of blood into the lungs, which explains <strong>orthopnoea</strong> in heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong> diverts blood to well-ventilated alveoli. When hypoxia is everywhere, it causes <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n";
